WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.354 --> 00:00:11.427
Tom Lindqvist: Morning, everyone. Thanks for making time. I know Q3 close is always busy on your side, so I'll keep us moving.

2
00:00:11.792 --> 00:00:17.976
Owen Price: Morning, Tom. We're good. Michelle's dialed in from the office, Brandon's on his headset somewhere.

3
00:00:18.248 --> 00:00:20.553
Brandon Hughes: I'm here. Can you hear me okay?

4
00:00:21.032 --> 00:00:30.315
Tom Lindqvist: Loud and clear. Let's start with the numbers, then I want to leave plenty of room for the stuff you flagged.

5
00:00:31.092 --> 00:00:31.943
Owen Price: Sounds good.

6
00:00:32.603 --> 00:00:43.805
Tom Lindqvist: So inbound sales calls for Q3 came in at about 41,200, that's up 7% over Q2. Fiber install calls were the biggest mover, roughly 12,800 of that total.

7
00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:54.391
Owen Price: Yeah, that lines up with what we saw on the media side. The fiber campaign in the Denver metro really carried the quarter.

8
00:00:55.357 --> 00:01:04.519
Tom Lindqvist: Attribution held up well too. About 68% of those calls tied back to a paid campaign or keyword, which is a couple points better than last quarter.

9
00:01:05.604 --> 00:01:12.289
Owen Price: The new attribution view by campaign is honestly the report my team opens first every morning.

10
00:01:13.185 --> 00:01:20.512
Tom Lindqvist: Good to hear. Anything you want changed on it, just tell me and I'll pass it along.

11
00:01:20.941 --> 00:01:24.138
Owen Price: Not right now. It's doing what we need.

12
00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:34.405
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. On the AI Voice Agent side, containment came in at 31% for the quarter. That's up from 24% in Q2.

13
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:38.217
Brandon Hughes: That's a real jump. What's driving it?

14
00:01:38.958 --> 00:01:46.481
Tom Lindqvist: Mostly the billing and hours-of-operation calls. Those are the two intents getting handled end to end without a transfer.

15
00:01:47.889 --> 00:01:54.305
Michelle Tran: That matches what I see in Genesys. The queue volume for general billing is down noticeably.

16
00:01:54.996 --> 00:02:01.810
Tom Lindqvist: Good. And messaging, the SMS follow-up after a missed call, that's running at about 4,100 conversations for the quarter.

17
00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:08.588
Brandon Hughes: We've had decent pickup on that. The sales team likes it because it keeps the lead warm.

18
00:02:09.371 --> 00:02:16.204
Tom Lindqvist: Okay, that's the summary. Michelle, you had something on the PreSense side you wanted to walk through?

19
00:02:17.421 --> 00:02:28.070
Michelle Tran: Yeah, this is the big one for me. Every time we transfer from sales to retention in Genesys, the PreSense data just doesn't come across. The second agent gets nothing.

20
00:02:28.921 --> 00:02:36.238
Tom Lindqvist: So the screen pop works on the first leg into sales, but on the warm transfer to retention it drops.

21
00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:45.607
Michelle Tran: Correct. The retention agent sees the caller ID and that's it. No journey, no pages viewed, nothing. They end up asking the customer to repeat everything.

22
00:02:46.109 --> 00:02:47.813
Tom Lindqvist: How often is this happening?

23
00:02:48.715 --> 00:02:56.230
Michelle Tran: It's consistent. Any time the call moves from the sales skill to retention. I'd say a few hundred calls a week.

24
00:02:56.968 --> 00:03:01.696
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. And this is the second time this quarter we've talked about transfer behavior.

25
00:03:02.663 --> 00:03:07.976
Michelle Tran: It is. I raised it in August and we didn't get to a fix.

26
00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:18.657
Tom Lindqvist: I remember. Let me get the specifics from you after this call, the exact skill names and a couple of example calls, and I'll take it to the team.

27
00:03:18.657 --> 00:03:22.328
Tom Lindqvist: I don't want to guess at the cause on the call.

28
00:03:23.171 --> 00:03:28.474
Michelle Tran: That works. I can pull two or three call IDs this afternoon.

29
00:03:28.843 --> 00:03:31.266
Tom Lindqvist: Perfect. Brandon, you had the other one.

30
00:03:31.924 --> 00:03:45.582
Brandon Hughes: The voice agent is handing people to a live rep and the rep has to ask for the account number again. That's the one thing we told customers they wouldn't have to do.

31
00:03:46.371 --> 00:03:51.787
Tom Lindqvist: So the handoff from the voice agent into the live queue isn't carrying the context over.

32
00:03:53.140 --> 00:04:03.540
Brandon Hughes: Right. The agent collected the account number, verified the caller, then transfers, and the rep starts from zero. It makes us look like we don't know who's calling.

33
00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.708
Tom Lindqvist: Is it every handoff or specific intents?

34
00:04:07.888 --> 00:04:15.644
Brandon Hughes: Mostly the ones that go to the billing queue. The sales handoffs seem better, but I haven't measured it closely.

35
00:04:15.895 --> 00:04:27.688
Tom Lindqvist: Okay. Can you send me a few examples too? Same as Michelle, call IDs and the time of day. I'll bring both of these to the product team together since they're related.

36
00:04:28.679 --> 00:04:31.379
Brandon Hughes: Yeah, I'll get you something by Friday.

37
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.477
Tom Lindqvist: Thanks. Owen, you mentioned procurement.

38
00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:45.465
Owen Price: Procurement is going to ask me why AI Voice Agent is priced per minute on top of the platform fee. I need a better answer than I have.

39
00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:50.839
Tom Lindqvist: Fair. Is this for the renewal planning or a separate review?

40
00:04:51.548 --> 00:04:58.875
Owen Price: It's the annual vendor review. They look at every line item and they've been on a cost-per-outcome kick all year.

41
00:04:59.791 --> 00:05:08.315
Tom Lindqvist: Got it. Let me pull the usage detail and put together something you can hand them. I'd rather give you real numbers than a talking point.

42
00:05:09.088 --> 00:05:13.412
Owen Price: That would help. They respond better to math than to a slide.

43
00:05:14.589 --> 00:05:18.371
Tom Lindqvist: Understood. I'll have that to you by early next week.

44
00:05:18.922 --> 00:05:19.722
Owen Price: Thanks.

45
00:05:21.131 --> 00:05:23.840
Tom Lindqvist: Anything else on your list before we wrap?

46
00:05:25.088 --> 00:05:29.369
Michelle Tran: The Google Ads and Adobe Analytics connections have been fine. No issues there.

47
00:05:30.625 --> 00:05:34.623
Brandon Hughes: Same for us. The Salesforce sync is doing what it should.

48
00:05:35.299 --> 00:05:46.586
Tom Lindqvist: Good. So action items: Michelle and Brandon send call examples, I take the transfer and handoff issues to the team, and I get Owen the pricing breakdown.

49
00:05:47.649 --> 00:05:48.830
Owen Price: That covers it.

50
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:52.994
Tom Lindqvist: Great. I'll send a recap this afternoon. Thanks, everyone.

51
00:05:54.162 --> 00:05:54.962
Michelle Tran: Thanks, Tom.

52
00:05:55.804 --> 00:05:56.604
Brandon Hughes: Talk soon.

53
00:05:57.118 --> 00:05:58.491
Owen Price: Have a good one.
